--TEST--
`[log] level` is the minimum level written, with the bound named on both sides: the last level a deployment drops and the first one it keeps — `rule:errors/log-level`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Log\Level
// `rule:errors/log-level`'s last paragraph. The directive names a floor, not a filter for
// one level, so the roster is walked past it in both directions: `Debug` and
// `Info` are quieter than `Warn` and are written nowhere, `Warn` is the level
// named and is written, and `Error` is louder and is written too. A floor
// implemented with § 2's syslog severities the wrong way round — they run
// downward, `Debug` at 7 and `Critical` at 2 — keeps the configured level and
// inverts everything either side of it, which is what asking about all four
// costs nothing and catches.
//
// The records are read back out of the target rather than off standard output,
// because a deployment that names a destination gets one: with `[log] target`
// set, `Core\Log::write` no longer writes the program's own stream. The hook
// runs as the last user code of the script (`rule:observability/script-on-exit`), by which point every
// record above has been written or dropped.
Core\Script::onExit(fn (): void => {
    echo Core\IO::readText("app.log");
});

Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Debug, "quieter than the floor");
Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Info, "the last level dropped");
Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Warn, "the floor itself");
Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Error, "above the floor");
--FILE nvs.toml--
[log]
target = "file:app.log"
level = "Warn"

[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
--EXPECT--
{"level":"warn","msg":"the floor itself","source":{"file":"case.nvs","line":23}}
{"level":"error","msg":"above the floor","source":{"file":"case.nvs","line":24}}
